        // blog-nrb-buying.html (article)
        ['blog-nrb-buying','hero','title','The NRB property-buying guide: from Dubai to Dhaka, safely'],
        ['blog-nrb-buying','hero','subtitle','NITA accounts, power of attorney, due diligence — a practical checklist before you wire money home.'],
        ['blog-nrb-buying','article','body','
    <div class="blog-meta" style="margin-bottom:24px"><span>📅 Jul 08, 2026</span><span>⏱ 8 min read</span><span>🏷 NRB · Buying</span></div>
    <p>Every year, thousands of non-resident Bangladeshis buy property from abroad — and a surprising number hit the same three walls: money that can\'t be repatriated, titles that aren\'t clear, and a caretaker who stops answering calls. This guide compresses ten years of NRB buying mistakes into a checklist.</p>
    <h2>1. Move money through NITA, not friends</h2>
    <p>Open a <b>Non-Resident Investor\'s Taka Account (NITA)</b> with an authorized dealer bank. Funds that arrive through NITA — and only those — carry a documented trail for later repatriation of rental income and sale proceeds. Money moved through friends or hundi cannot be legally taken back out.</p>
    <h2>2. Due diligence before the token money</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Verify the seller\'s title at the sub-registry (dip into the mutation and khatian records).</li>
      <li>Check the property against the building\'s holding number and city corporation assessment.</li>
      <li>Confirm no government acquisition order is pending on the plot.</li>
      <li>Ask for the last utility bills — unpaid dues become yours after transfer.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>3. Power of attorney that actually works</h2>
    <p>An NRB usually buys through a general power of attorney (GPA) holder. A GPA registered in Bangladesh is enforceable here; a notarized document from abroad must be attested by the Bangladesh mission before it carries weight. Keep the original deed and its certified copy in separate places.</p>
    <div class="callout">The ৳30,00,000 mistake: one Dubai-based buyer paid token money on a flat whose owner had already pledged the deed to a bank. Verification of the encumbrance certificate (EC) at the sub-registry — a 30-minute check — would have stopped it.</div>
    <h2>4. Plan the exit before you enter</h2>
    <p>Repatriation works when the trail is clean: rental income is sent via NITA, TDS is deducted and deposited, and the annual return is filed with the NBR showing the income. Owners who skip the return find their remittance blocked at the bank\'s compliance desk.</p>
    <h2>How KRTaker helps from abroad</h2>
    <p>KRTaker stores deeds, ECs and NITA details per property; KR reminds you before compliance deadlines, tracks rental income against your NITA account, and keeps your local team accountable with a visible activity log — so "the caretaker stopped answering" stops being a risk.</p>
    <p><a href="for-nrb.html">NRB features →</a></p>
  '],
        ['seo','blog-nrb-buying','meta_title','The NRB property-buying guide: from Dubai to Dhaka, safely — KRTaker'],
        ['seo','blog-nrb-buying','meta_description','NITA accounts, power of attorney, due diligence — a practical checklist before you wire money home.'],
        // blog-nrb-remittance.html (article)
        ['blog-nrb-remittance','hero','title','NRB guide: bringing rental income home'],
        ['blog-nrb-remittance','hero','subtitle','NRTA/NITA, AD banks, and the dossier that makes repatriation painless.'],
        ['blog-nrb-remittance','article','body','
    <div class="blog-meta" style="margin-bottom:24px"><span>📅 Jun 12, 2026</span><span>⏱ 7 min read</span><span>🏷 NRB · Remittance</span></div>
    <p>You own property in Bangladesh. You live in Dubai, London, New York. The rent is collected, the taka sits idle — and getting it out feels like a compliance maze. It doesn\'t have to be.</p>
    <h2>The two accounts</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>NRTA — Non-Resident Taka Account:</b> your local account for taka income while abroad.</li>
      <li><b>NITA — Non-Resident Investor\'s Taka Account:</b> for investment-linked funds, including property sale proceeds.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Under Bangladesh Bank guidelines, <b>house rent</b> and <b>sale proceeds of property</b> (with documentary evidence) are <b>permitted credits</b> to these accounts. The bottleneck is never the rule — it\'s the paperwork.</p>
    <h2>The dossier AD branches expect</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Registered lease agreement(s) proving the tenancy</li>
      <li>Tax clearance certificate (TCC)</li>
      <li>Form IT10B — return of assets &amp; liabilities</li>
      <li>Signed rent receipts for the period</li>
      <li>Form TM for outward remittances (and QA-22 for foreign nationals)</li>
    </ol>
    <h2>Where it breaks</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Leases that were never registered (see our <a href="blog-lease-registration.html">registration post</a>) — no valid lease, no credit.</li>
      <li>Receipts the tenant "forgot" to sign.</li>
      <li>Scattered documents your bank asks you to re-assemble every quarter.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="callout">💡 <b>KRTaker\'s NRB module</b> keeps every document current automatically — registered leases, signed receipts, IT10B-ready figures — and exports the dossier in one click for your AD branch.</div>
    <h2>Bottom line</h2>
    <p>Repatriation isn\'t blocked by law; it\'s blocked by documentation. Let the platform keep yours perfect.</p>
    <p><a href="for-nrb.html">NRB investor hub →</a></p>
  '],
        ['seo','blog-nrb-remittance','meta_title','NRB guide: bringing rental income home — KRTaker'],
        ['seo','blog-nrb-remittance','meta_description','NRTA/NITA, AD banks, and the dossier that makes repatriation painless.'],
        // blog-tds-commercial.html (article)
        ['blog-tds-commercial','hero','title','TDS on commercial rent: the 4% rule nobody invoices for'],
        ['blog-tds-commercial','hero','subtitle','Section 128 of the Income Tax Act 2023 makes every commercial tenant a collecting agent — most don\'t know it.'],
        ['blog-tds-commercial','article','body','
    <div class="blog-meta" style="margin-bottom:24px"><span>📅 Jul 02, 2026</span><span>⏱ 6 min read</span><span>🏷 Tax · TDS</span></div>
    <p>If you rent an office, shop or showroom in Bangladesh, the Income Tax Act 2023 puts a withholding duty on you — not on the landlord. Section 128 requires commercial tenants to deduct <b>4%</b> from monthly rent and deposit it with the government. Here is how it works, and why most tenants discover it only during an audit.</p>
    <h2>Who withholds, at what rate</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Commercial rent — 4%</b> deducted at source by the tenant (§128).</li>
      <li><b>Residential rent — 10%</b> when annual rent crosses ৳36,00,000 (§109).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Both are advances on the landlord\'s tax liability. The tenant files the withholding via a challan and issues a <b>TDS certificate (Form 12BB class)</b> to the landlord, who claims it as a credit at assessment.</p>
    <div class="callout">Worked example: a Gulshan showroom rents at ৳4,50,000/mo. The tenant must deduct <b>৳18,000</b> every month (4%) and deposit it by the 7th of the following month. Net rent paid to the owner: ৳4,32,000.</div>
    <h2>What goes wrong in practice</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Tenant pays full rent and never deducts — the whole year\'s TDS becomes the tenant\'s own liability, plus interest and penalty.</li>
      <li>Landlord\'s return doesn\'t show rental income but tenant filed TDS — triggering a mismatch notice and a routine inquiry.</li>
      <li>Advance rent or security deposits are sometimes treated as rent; only refundable deposits are outside the TDS net.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>How KRTaker handles it</h2>
    <p>Every commercial lease in KRTaker carries a TDS flag. Invoices are generated at gross rent, TDS is computed at 4%, and the net payable is what the tenant sees. At month-end, KR produces the exact challan figures per tenant — so your accounts team stops reconciling spreadsheets.</p>
    <p><a href="tools.html">Run the TDS calculator →</a></p>
  '],
        ['seo','blog-tds-commercial','meta_title','TDS on commercial rent: the 4% rule nobody invoices for — KRTaker'],
        ['seo','blog-tds-commercial','meta_description','Section 128 of the Income Tax Act 2023 makes every commercial tenant a collecting agent — most don\'t know it.'],
        // terms.html (article)
        ['terms','hero','title','Terms of service'],
        ['terms','hero','subtitle','Last updated: June 2026'],
        ['terms','article','body','
    <h2>1. The service</h2>
    <p>KRTaker ("the Platform") provides property-management software and an AI assistant ("KR") for users in Bangladesh, including property owners (Subscribers), service partners, legal consultants, tenants and platform staff. This agreement governs your use of the Platform.</p>
    <h2>2. Accounts &amp; roles</h2>
    <p>You are responsible for the accuracy of registration details and for all activity under your account. Role-based access (Owner, Manager, Tenant, Service Partner, Service Manager, Legal, CRM, Accountant, HR) defines what each user may view and do. You must not share credentials or access data outside your role\'s scope.</p>
    <h2>3. Subscriptions &amp; billing</h2>
    <p>Plans (Starter, Business, Enterprise) renew monthly or annually. Payments are processed via bKash, SSLCommerz or bank transfer. Fees are non-refundable except where required by law. Service partners pay a 10% platform fee per completed work order, net of applicable TDS.</p>
    <h2>4. Legal &amp; compliance information</h2>
    <p>The Platform\'s compliance engine, calculations and AI answers are <b>informational tools</b>, not legal advice. Statutory requirements change; verify critical matters with a licensed professional. KRTaker is not a party to leases and is not liable for disputes between owners and tenants.</p>
    <h2>5. Acceptable use</h2>
    <p>You may not use the Platform to violate Bangladeshi law, infringe rights, or attempt to access another tenant\'s data. Digital lockouts and unlawful evictions are strictly prohibited by our system and our terms.</p>
    <h2>6. Data &amp; security</h2>
    <p>We protect data with encryption in transit and at rest, and enforce multi-tenant isolation. You retain ownership of your data; you may export it at any time. See our <a href="privacy.html">Privacy Policy</a>.</p>
    <h2>7. Liability</h2>
    <p>To the maximum extent permitted by law, KRTaker\'s aggregate liability is limited to fees paid in the preceding 3 months. We are not liable for indirect, incidental or consequential damages, including lost rent or legal outcomes.</p>
    <h2>8. Termination</h2>
    <p>Either party may terminate with 30 days\' notice. On termination you may export your data within 30 days; thereafter it is deleted per our retention policy.</p>
    <h2>9. Changes</h2>
    <p>We may update these terms with 14 days\' notice. Continued use constitutes acceptance.</p>
    <h2>10. Contact</h2>
    <p>Questions: <a href="contact.html">contact us</a> or legal@krtaker.com.</p>
  '],
        ['seo','terms','meta_title','Terms of service — KRTaker'],
        ['seo','terms','meta_description','Last updated: June 2026'],
        // privacy.html (article)
        ['privacy','hero','title','Privacy policy'],
        ['privacy','hero','subtitle','Last updated: June 2026'],
        ['privacy','article','body','
    <h2>1. What we collect</h2>
    <p><b>Account data:</b> name, organization, email, phone, plan. <b>Portfolio data:</b> properties, units, leases, tenants, invoices, receipts, maintenance records, khatian references — the data you enter to run your business. <b>Usage data:</b> pages visited, features used, device type.</p>
    <h2>2. How we use it</h2>
    <p>To operate the Platform, compute taxes and compliance checks, process payments via our gateways (bKash, SSLCommerz), provide the AI caretaker, send billing/statutory reminders, and improve the service. We do not sell your data.</p>
    <h2>3. Multi-tenant isolation</h2>
    <p>Your portfolio data is isolated at the database level. Owners never see other owners\' data; tenants only see their own unit and lease; partners only see their assigned works. Staff roles access data only within their function and are audited.</p>
    <h2>4. AI processing</h2>
    <p>When you chat with KR, your query and relevant portfolio context are processed to generate answers and actions. Tool actions are logged and shown to you. We configure model providers (e.g. DeepSeek) with no-training agreements; your data is not used to train public models.</p>
    <h2>5. Payments</h2>
    <p>Payment credentials are handled by our payment partners (bKash, SSLCommerz). We never store your card numbers or wallet PINs. Transaction references are recorded for reconciliation.</p>
    <h2>6. Retention &amp; export</h2>
    <p>You can export your data (PDF/XLSX) any time from the workspace. On account deletion, data is erased within 90 days except where law requires retention (e.g. financial records).</p>
    <h2>7. Security</h2>
    <p>We use TLS encryption in transit, AES-256 at rest, role-based access control, and regular security reviews. No system is perfectly secure; you share information at your own risk, and we ask you to use strong passwords.</p>
    <h2>8. Your rights</h2>
    <p>You may access, correct, export or delete your personal data by contacting privacy@krtaker.com. We respond within 30 days.</p>
    <h2>9. Cookies &amp; local storage</h2>
    <p>We use local storage for session and preferences. Marketing pages may use analytics cookies; you can decline via the consent banner.</p>
    <h2>10. Contact</h2>
    <p>Privacy questions: privacy@krtaker.com · <a href="contact.html">contact page</a></p>
  '],
